#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Stream Fyers order and position updates over a websocket and merge them into the Redis hashes `fyers:orders:orders` and
`fyers:portfolio:positions`, and keep streaming until stopped.

    bin/fyers/orders/websocket_order_details
    redis-cli HGETALL fyers:orders:orders
    redis-cli HGET fyers:orders:orders <order id>
    redis-cli HGETALL fyers:portfolio:positions

The connection and the login are `FyersOrderUpdatesSocket` and `FyersSession` in `stock_brokers/websockets/fyers.py`,
whose module docstring describes the handshake and the messages at `wss://socket.fyers.in/trade/v3`. This script
runs the socket and turns the orders and positions it hands over into the Redis writes below, so it needs no other
feed process or key.

**The stream.** The session is authenticated during the websocket handshake, by an `authorization` header of
the app id and access token joined by a colon, so a bad credential fails the HTTP upgrade itself. Once open it
sends `{"T": "SUB_ORD", "SLIST": ["orders", "positions"], "SUB_T": 1}`, Fyers answers with code 1605, and from
then on every change arrives as JSON: `{"s": "ok", "orders": {...}}` for an order - `id`, `id_exchange`,
`org_ord_status` (1 cancelled, 2 filled, 4 transit, 5 rejected, 6 pending), `symbol`, `exchange`, `segment`,
`fy_token`, `tran_side` (1 buy, -1 sell), `ord_type`, `product_type`, `validity`, `qty`, `qty_filled`,
`qty_remaining`, `price_limit`, `price_stop`, `price_traded`, `time_oms`, `oms_msg`, `ordertag` - and
`{"s": "ok", "positions": {...}}` for a position - `symbol`, `fy_token`, `exchange`, `segment`, `product_type`,
`buy_qty`, `sell_qty`, `net_qty`, `buy_avg`, `sell_avg`, `net_avg`, `buy_val`, `sell_val`, `cf_buy_qty`,
`cf_sell_qty`, `pl_realized`, `pl_unrealized`, `pl_total`, `qty_multiplier`. The trades channel is not
subscribed.

**The keys.** Each is the one hash for its kind, shared with a REST poller:

- `fyers:orders:orders`, keyed by the order `id`, shared with `bin/fyers/orders/api_order_details`, which merges the polled order book.
- `fyers:portfolio:positions`, keyed `symbol:product` as Fyers spells both (`NSE:INFY-EQ:INTRADAY`), since one symbol
  can be held under more than one product, shared with `bin/fyers/portfolio/positions`, which merges the polled positions. The
  update's `symbol` and `product_type` are the same values as the REST row's `symbol` and `productType`, so a
  position lands on one field whichever wrote it.

Each update is written as `{"observed_at": <epoch received>, "source": "websocket", "order" | "position": <normalized>,
"data": <Fyers' object exactly as sent>}`. The normalized `order` and `position` have the same fields the REST pollers
build, mapped from the update's own names - see `bin/fyers/orders/api_order_details` and `bin/fyers/portfolio/positions` for the field tables. Two
order fields only the update carries, `cancelled_quantity` and `exchange_timestamp`, and one position field only the
REST positions carry, `last_price`, is null from the other side. A websocket update always replaces its entry; a polled
row replaces it only when the entry was observed before the poll's request was sent. Each hash expires at 06:00 IST,
moved to the next 06:00 by every write.

**The streams.** Every update is also appended, in arrival order, to a Redis Stream as
`{"timestamp": <received, local wall clock>, "data": <Fyers' object>}` - so the history the hashes overwrite is kept there: each order update to `fyers:order-updates:stream` as an `update` field,
which `bin/fyers/orders/store_orders_to_db` writes to `fyers.order_updates`, and each position update to
`fyers:positions_updates:stream` as a `position` field, which `bin/fyers/portfolio/store_positions_to_db` writes to
`fyers.positions`. Each is capped at about 20,000 entries.

**Sessions.** The token is read afresh on every connect, so a login made by any process is picked up. A token
whose expiry has passed, a handshake refused with HTTP 401 or 403, an error message carrying a session code
(-8, -15, -16, -17), or a socket that fails to open six times running logs in again - confirmed against the
profile, and without checking whether another process has already replaced the token - before reconnecting; refused again straight after that login, it gives up
rather than keep sending refused requests, which Cloudflare punishes with an address ban. A reconnect otherwise
backs off from 1 to 60 seconds. Updates arrive only when an order or position changes, so a quiet socket is
not a broken one.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and 1 when a login fails or the socket still cannot open after
logging in again. Reads only - it never places, modifies or cancels an order.
"""

import sys
import json
import signal
import threading
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from pathlib import Path
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

